---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE, AUTHOR, COMPANY, BENCH } from '../data/site';

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'AboutPage',
    name: 'About HailBrowser',
    description:
      'HailBrowser is an open-source browser-use agent built by Shyamnath Sankar at GenrecAI: an MIT extension for the Chrome you are already signed into, plus an MCP server for coding agents.',
    url: `${SITE.url}/about`,
    isPartOf: { '@id': `${SITE.url}/#site` },
    mainEntity: {
      '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
      name: 'HailBrowser',
      applicationCategory: 'BrowserApplication',
      operatingSystem: 'Chrome, Edge, Brave and other Chromium browsers 116+',
      url: SITE.url,
      codeRepository: SITE.repo,
      license: 'https://opensource.org/license/mit',
      softwareVersion: SITE.version,
      author: { '@type': 'Person', name: AUTHOR.name, url: AUTHOR.url },
      publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: COMPANY.name, url: COMPANY.url },
    },
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: `${SITE.url}/` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'About', item: `${SITE.url}/about` },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="About HailBrowser — built by Shyamnath Sankar, GenrecAI"
  description="HailBrowser is an MIT browser-use agent for the Chrome you are signed into. Built by Shyamnath Sankar at GenrecAI. No account, no telemetry."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <div class="frame">
    <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span aria-current="page">About</span>
    </nav>

    <header class="page-hero">
      <div class="page-hero__meta">
        <span class="pill pill--ink">About</span>
        <span class="pill">MIT · v{SITE.version}</span>
      </div>
      <h1>A browser agent<br />that stays yours.</h1>
      <p>
        HailBrowser is built on one bet: the agent should come to your browser, not the other way round.
        It is an MIT-licensed extension for the Chrome you already use, with a local MCP server so your
        coding agent can use the same signed-in session. It is made by
        <a class="link" href={AUTHOR.url}>{AUTHOR.name}</a> at
        <a class="link" href={COMPANY.url}>{COMPANY.name}</a>.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="section section--flush">
      <div class="stats">
        <div class="stat">
          <b>MIT</b>
          <span>Licence</span>
          <em>Read it, fork it, ship it. No seat price.</em>
        </div>
        <div class="stat">
          <b>0</b>
          <span>Telemetry</span>
          <em>No analytics, no account, no call-home.</em>
        </div>
        <div class="stat">
          <b>127.0.0.1</b>
          <span>Where the hub listens</span>
          <em>Not a cloud relay. Your machine only.</em>
        </div>
        <div class="stat">
          <b>{BENCH.passed}/{BENCH.total}</b>
          <span>Benchmark tasks passed</span>
          <em>At a {BENCH.avg} average, in the open.</em>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="split split--wide-right">
        <div class="stack">
          <p class="label">Why it exists</p>
          <h2 class="display-l">The browser is<br />already logged in.</h2>
          <p class="body-muted">
            Most browser agents solve the wrong half of the problem. They can click; the hard part was
            never clicking. It is everything around it: the session, the extension, the tab you were
            already on, the account you cannot sign into twice.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            So HailBrowser starts from the browser rather than from a script. It attaches to the Chrome,
            Edge or Brave you are in, reads the page into something a model can reason about, and does
            the action in the tab you already have open. There is no second profile to keep in sync, no
            cookies to export, no fresh Chromium that has never seen your bank or your inbox.
          </p>
          <p>
            The second bet is that speed is a design decision, not a model upgrade. Fast mode asks the
            model for one thing — an operation and an element index — and never lets model output become
            a selector, a coordinate or code. That is why the benchmark loop averages {BENCH.avg} across
            {BENCH.total} tasks, and why the harness is published rather than quoted.
          </p>
          <p>
            The third is that an agent people cannot inspect is an agent they will not trust with a real
            login. Every step is listed, every driven tab is outlined, there is a Stop button, and the
            whole extension is plain ES modules you can read. Limits are documented on a page of their
            own instead of discovered in a support thread.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div>
          <p class="label">Principles</p>
          <h2 class="display-m">Four things that do not change.</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="body-muted">
          Features move. These are the constraints the features have to fit inside.
        </p>
      </div>

      <dl class="deflist">
        <div>
          <dt>Your browser, not ours</dt>
          <dd>
            The agent runs where you already work. A separate browser is a separate set of logins,
            extensions and habits, and we are not asking anyone to move.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Your keys stay local</dt>
          <dd>
            Model keys live in <code>chrome.storage.local</code> and go only to the provider you chose.
            The MCP hub binds to <code>127.0.0.1:12306</code> and rejects web-page origins. There is no
            HailBrowser account and no telemetry.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Claims come with a harness</dt>
          <dd>
            “Fastest” is a measured statement, so the {BENCH.total}-task harness, the per-task times and
            the command to rerun it are all public. If a number cannot be reproduced, it does not go on
            the site.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Say what it cannot do</dt>
          <dd>
            Shadow DOM, cross-origin iframes, canvas apps, file uploads and PDF research are unfinished.
            The <a class="link" href="/docs/limits">limits page</a> says so before you install, not after.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div>
          <p class="label">How it is built</p>
          <h2 class="display-m">Small enough to read.</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="body-muted">
          No bundler, no framework, no account service. The interesting parts are the page snapshot, the
          decision loop and the local hub.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="data">
        <table>
          <caption>The stack, briefly</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Part</th><th scope="col">What it is</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Extension</td><td>Manifest V3, plain ES modules, no build step. Side panel plus content scripts.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Page access</td><td><code>chrome.debugger</code> on your normal profile. No remote-debugging port, no second browser.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Decision loop</td><td>Chat, Fast (ultrafast), Research and Tasks, over any OpenAI-compatible provider.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>MCP server</td><td>A local hub on <code>127.0.0.1:12306</code>, {SITE.mcpToolCount} browser tools over MCP, {SITE.toolCount} in total including task runs.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Benchmark</td><td>26 local pages, scored on page state or answer, run with <code>scripts/bench-fast.mjs</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Requirements</td><td>Chromium {SITE.minChrome}+, Node for the MCP server, a model key unless you run a local model.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="author">
        <div class="author__who">
          <p class="label">Built by</p>
          <b>{AUTHOR.name}</b>
          <span>
            {AUTHOR.role} of HailBrowser, at {COMPANY.name}. The extension, the MCP server, the docs and
            the benchmark are maintained in the open; the repository is the source of truth for every
            claim on this site.
          </span>
        </div>
        <div class="author__links">
          <a class="btn btn--ghost btn--sm" href={AUTHOR.url} rel="noopener" target="_blank">Personal site <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
          <a class="btn btn--ghost btn--sm" href={COMPANY.url} rel="noopener" target="_blank">{COMPANY.name} <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
          <a class="btn btn--ghost btn--sm" href={SITE.repo} rel="noopener" target="_blank">GitHub <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section section--tight">
      <div class="callout">
        <p class="label">Get involved</p>
        <p>
          Bug reports and reproducible benchmark runs are the two most useful contributions. The
          <a class="link" href="/support">support page</a> says what to include; the
          <a class="link" href="/docs/install">install guide</a> gets you to a working build.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section section--tight">
      <nav class="more-links" aria-label="Related">
        <a href="/benchmarks">
          <span class="label">Evidence</span>
          <b>Benchmark</b>
          <em>26 tasks, 4.2s each, every number.</em>
        </a>
        <a href="/browser-use">
          <span class="label">Category</span>
          <b>Browser use</b>
          <em>What the term means, and the two shapes.</em>
        </a>
        <a href="/comet-alternative">
          <span class="label">Compare</span>
          <b>Open-source Comet</b>
          <em>An extension, not a new browser.</em>
        </a>
        <a href="/docs/security">
          <span class="label">Trust</span>
          <b>Security model</b>
          <em>Keys, localhost, and untrusted pages.</em>
        </a>
      </nav>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
